---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.tsx";
import CopyCommand from "../components/CopyCommand.tsx";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

// The page the MCP server's listings and posts point at.
const REPO = "https://github.com/PunkABeat/hostbeam-mcp";
const NPM = "https://www.npmjs.com/package/@hostbeam/mcp";
const CLAUDE = "claude mcp add -s user hostbeam -- npx -y @hostbeam/mcp";
const CODEX = "codex mcp add hostbeam -- npx -y @hostbeam/mcp";
const JSON_CONFIG = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "hostbeam": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@hostbeam/mcp"]
    }
  }
}`;
---

<Base
  title="Hostbeam for coding agents"
  description="Install Hostbeam's MCP server on your host and your coding agent reads the screenshots you beam. No path to paste. Works with Claude Code, Codex and any MCP client."
>
  <div class="site" id="top">
    <Nav />
    <main class="wrap doc">
      <h1 class="h2">Let the agent open the screenshot itself</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Hostbeam beams a screenshot from your Mac or your iPhone to your server and puts its path
        on your clipboard. The MCP server removes that last step: your agent asks for the screenshot and reads it, so there's
        nothing to paste.
      </p>

      <div
        class="term demo"
        aria-label="A terminal session where a coding agent reads a beamed screenshot through Hostbeam's MCP server"
      >
        <div><span class="t-dim">dev@prod-box:~/app$</span> claude</div>
        <div><span class="t-dim">›</span> The layout breaks on mobile. Look at the screenshot I just beamed.</div>
        <div><span class="t-beam">● hostbeam · latest_screenshot</span></div>
        <div><span class="t-land">✓</span> I see it. The nav wraps under 390px. Fixing.</div>
      </div>

      <section>
        <h2>Install it on the host</h2>
        <p>Install it on the server your agent runs on, not on your Mac. It needs Node 18 or later.</p>

        <h3>Claude Code</h3>
        <div class="cmd"><CopyCommand command={CLAUDE} client:visible /></div>
        <p class="aside"><code class="mono">-s user</code> makes it available in every project on that host.</p>

        <h3>Codex</h3>
        <div class="cmd"><CopyCommand command={CODEX} client:visible /></div>

        <h3>Any other MCP client</h3>
        <pre class="term config">{JSON_CONFIG}</pre>

        <p>
          If the host can't reach npm, copy <code class="mono">hostbeam-mcp.mjs</code> from
          <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a> and run it with <code class="mono">node</code>. It has no
          dependencies. If you changed Hostbeam's folder for that host, give the server the same
          folder with <code class="mono">HOSTBEAM_DIR</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Then just ask</h2>
        <p>
          Beam a screenshot and ask the way you'd ask a person: “what does the screenshot I just
          sent show?” The agent fetches your latest beam, with every file in it if you sent several,
          and knows how long ago it arrived.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What the agent can use</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><code class="mono">latest_screenshot</code>: your most recent beam, as images.</li>
          <li><code class="mono">list_screenshots</code>: recent beams with names, sizes and times, without image data.</li>
          <li><code class="mono">read_screenshot</code>: one screenshot by name.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What it won't do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Read anything except the screenshots Hostbeam beamed into that one folder.</li>
          <li>
            Read a file that isn't a picture. A PDF or a log you beam lands in the same folder, and
            the path Hostbeam copies gives the agent its location to open with its own tools.
          </li>
          <li>Write, move or delete a file.</li>
          <li>Follow a symlink, or read a folder that belongs to another user on the host.</li>
          <li>
            Hand a model an image it can't accept. A HEIC photo, or a file over 3.75 MB, comes back as
            its path.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>If it doesn't work</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>The agent says there's no screenshot.</b> Check that
            <code class="mono">claude mcp list</code> or <code class="mono">codex mcp list</code> shows
            <code class="mono">hostbeam</code>, then ask the agent to use Hostbeam's latest screenshot.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Codex misses it right after opening.</b> <code class="mono">npx</code> takes a few
            seconds to start the server, so wait a moment before your first message.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>“Belongs to another user.”</b> On a shared host, someone else created that folder first.
            Pick a folder of your own in Hostbeam, and set the same folder as
            <code class="mono">HOSTBEAM_DIR</code>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Start a beam from a script</h2>
        <p>
          The agent reads beams. Something else can start one: a shell script, a launcher, a
          Shortcut, or the agent itself through a shell.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <code class="mono">open hostbeam://beam</code> beams the screenshot on your clipboard,
            exactly as the shortcut does.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code class="mono">open "hostbeam://host?id=my-server"</code> changes which host beams go
            to, using the name you gave it in Hostbeam.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code class="mono">open -a Hostbeam shot.png</code> beams a file. This one needs no
            permission and always works.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The two links work out of the box, and one switch turns them off: Preferences → General
          → Beaming → “Let other apps control Hostbeam”. If a link is ever ignored, that switch is
          the place to look. A link can only ever choose between hosts you have already added, and
          a web page cannot open one without your browser asking first. Needs Hostbeam 0.1.26 or
          newer.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Free and open source</h2>
        <p>
          The server is one MIT-licensed file, on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a> and
          <a href={NPM}>npm</a>. It costs nothing, and beaming works as it always has, licensed or
          not.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .doc {
    max-width: 720px;
    padding-top: 64px;
    padding-bottom: 96px;
  }
  .doc h1 {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.5vw, 48px);
  }
  .doc .lede {
    margin-top: 20px;
  }
  .doc .demo {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
  .doc section {
    margin-top: 44px;
  }
  .doc h2 {
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .doc h3 {
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 24px 0 0;
  }
  .doc p,
  .doc ul {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .doc ul {
    padding-left: 20px;
  }
  .doc li + li {
    margin-top: 6px;
  }
  .doc b {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .doc a {
    color: var(--beam);
  }
  .doc code.mono {
    font-size: 0.92em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .doc .cmd {
    margin-top: 10px;
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .doc .aside {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }
  .doc .config {
    margin: 10px 0 0;
    white-space: pre;
    overflow-x: auto;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
